{
  "id": "a1-05",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 6,
  "title": "The Three Conjugations",
  "summary": "The present tense completed: the second and third conjugations beside the first, how the stem is got from the principal parts, the verbs of wanting and being able with their infinitives, and the adverbs of frequency that go with the present.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "present-stem",
      "number": "35",
      "heading": "Getting the Present Stem",
      "statement": "Every present form is built on the **present stem**, which is got by removing the ending of the third person present — the second of the three principal parts (¶23). *dirba* gives the stem *dirb-*, *turi* gives *tur-*, *skaito* gives *skait-*. Because the third person is cited in every dictionary, no present form ever has to be guessed.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "dirbti, dirba, dirbo → dirb-",
          "gloss": "to work",
          "note": "first conjugation"
        },
        {
          "target": "turėti, turi, turėjo → tur-",
          "gloss": "to have",
          "note": "second conjugation"
        },
        {
          "target": "skaityti, skaito, skaitė → skait-",
          "gloss": "to read",
          "note": "third conjugation"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "conjugations",
        "present-conj-i"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "present-conj-ii",
      "number": "36",
      "heading": "The Second Conjugation",
      "statement": "Verbs whose third person present ends in **-i** take the endings below. The first person singular is **-iu**, not *-u*; and the second person singular is identical to the third, so that *tu turi* and *jis turi* differ only in their pronoun — one of the few places where the pronoun cannot be dropped without loss.",
