    {
      "id": "wanting-liking",
      "number": "39",
      "heading": "Wanting and Liking, and the Cases They Govern",
      "statement": "Two very common verbs differ in the case they take, and the difference must be learned with them. **Norėti**, to want, governs the **genitive**; **mėgti**, to like, governs the **accusative**. The reason is the old one: what is wanted is not yet had, and so is a part rather than a whole, while what is liked is liked entire.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Noriu kavos.",
          "gloss": "I want coffee.",
          "note": "genitive after norėti"
        },
        {
          "target": "Mėgstu kavą.",
          "gloss": "I like coffee.",
          "note": "accusative after mėgti"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji nori duonos, bet mėgsta obuolius.",
          "gloss": "She wants bread, but likes apples."
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "*klausyti*, to listen to, likewise governs the genitive: *klausau muzikos*, I am listening to music."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-quantity",
        "accusative-nouns"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "complementary-infinitive",
      "number": "40",
      "heading": "The Complementary Infinitive",
      "statement": "**Galėti** (to be able), **norėti** (to want) and **mėgti** (to like) may be completed by an **infinitive** instead of by a noun. The infinitive ends in *-ti* and never changes; the finite verb alone carries the person. Note that when *norėti* is completed by an infinitive there is no noun to put in the genitive, and the question of case does not arise.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš galiu dirbti.",
          "gloss": "I can work."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji nori skaityti.",
          "gloss": "She wants to read."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mes mėgstame dainuoti.",
          "gloss": "We like to sing."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ar jūs galite kalbėti lietuviškai?",
          "gloss": "Can you speak Lithuanian?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "wanting-liking",
        "conjugations"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "frequency-adverbs",
      "number": "41",
      "heading": "Adverbs of Frequency",
      "statement": "Because Lithuanian has one present tense where English has two (¶24), how often an action happens is said with an **adverb** rather than with a change of tense: *visada* (always), *dažnai* (often), *kartais* (sometimes), *retai* (rarely), *niekada* (never). They stand before the verb. **Niekada** requires the verb to be negated as well — Lithuanian doubles its negatives where English refuses to.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš dažnai skaitau.",
          "gloss": "I often read."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji kartais dirba namuose.",
          "gloss": "She sometimes works at home."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mes niekada nevalgome duonos.",
          "gloss": "We never eat bread.",
          "note": "niekada and ne- together"
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "The double negative is obligatory, not emphatic: *niekada valgome* is simply ungrammatical."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "negation",
        "present-conj-i"
      ],
      "core": true
    }
  ],
